// ParseError: error type returned when a numeric string conversion fails
//
// Usage:
//   - The std.core.types parse family (I8..I128/Isize/U8..U128/Usize and
//     F16/F32/F64/F128 .parse) returns Throw<_, ParseError>:
//       Malformed — the input is not a valid number literal for the type
//                   (empty, bad sign position, non-digit, trailing junk)
//       Overflow  — syntactically a number but beyond the type's range
//                   (floats: magnitude rounds to infinity; float underflow
//                   is NOT an error — it rounds to 0 or a subnormal per
//                   IEEE 754)
//     These used to return null — an out-of-range input was indistinguish-
//     able from a typo, and before that integers wrapped silently.
//   - Field semantics (aligned with the error family):
//       kind   : error category (exhaustive ADT)
//       message: human readable description
//       value  : the input string that triggered the error
//
// As a builtin error_newtype, it automatically gets the Error trait's
// message() default implementation (reads the message field).
// type_name() override returns "parse error" for easy identification in logs.
type ParseError: Err = ParseError(
    pub kind: ParseErrorKind,
    pub message: str,
    pub value: str,
) {
    override fun type_name(): str { "parse error" }
}

// Number-parse error categories (exhaustive ADT)
type ParseErrorKind =
    | Malformed   // not a valid number literal for the type
    | Overflow    // value beyond the type's representable range
